{"url":"https://www.reddit.com/r/NoStupidQuestions/comments/3zk9yn/how_do_american_school_buses_work/","title":"r/NoStupidQuestions on Reddit: How do American school buses work?","description":"There are many buses per school so <strong>routes are divided based on locations to ensure all the kids are picked up at a reasonable time</strong> (many areas have limits for how long a route can actually be).","extra_snippets":["You see it on TV, those clunky yellow buses seem to pick kids up from outside their house and take them to school. Is this how it actually works? How do they know where the kids live, what if they are sick, what if they live in the middle of nowhere, how is the route all worked out?","Why do they have such a system anyway, I am in the UK and we had to use the local bus network (who did put on a double decker for the morning / afternoon slot) with its usual route. Either that or walk, cycle or get a lift. If you are in an area with no buses - tough. It used to cost me 11p to get to school by the way.","There are many buses per school so routes are divided based on locations to ensure all the kids are picked up at a reasonable time (many areas have limits for how long a route can actually be). It's similar to how your local buses are routed with predetermined paths to take and times to be there.","If the kid is sick they simply don't show up at the bus stop and the bus continues on its route. If they live in the middle of nowhere they will still generally have access to a bus route with other kids near them (each school has a specific range they have kids from."]}
{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/School_bus","title":"School bus - Wikipedia","description":"In the United States and Canada, ... and <strong>operator</strong> specifications, with the largest designs seating up to 90 passengers. In both public and private education systems, other types of <strong>school</strong> buses are used for purposes of student transport outside of regular route <strong>service</strong>...","extra_snippets":["In the United States and Canada, bus bodies are restricted to a maximum width of 102 in (2.59 m) and a maximum length of 45 ft (13.7 m). Seating capacity is affected by both body length and operator specifications, with the largest designs seating up to 90 passengers. In both public and private education systems, other types of school buses are used for purposes of student transport outside of regular route service.","While school bus operations vary widely by location, in the United States and Canada, school bus services operate independent of public transport, with their own bus stops and schedules, coordinated with school class times.","A school bus is any type of bus owned, leased, contracted to, or operated by a school or school district. It is regularly used to transport students to and from school or school-related activities, but not including a charter bus or transit bus. Various configurations of school buses are used ...","While technically named \"National School Bus Glossy Yellow\", school bus yellow was adopted for use since it was considered easiest to see in dawn and dusk, and it contrasted well with black lettering. While not universally used worldwide, yellow has become the shade most commonly associated with school buses both in North America and abroad. During World War II, school bus manufacturers converted to military production, manufacturing buses and license-built trucks for the military. Following the war, school bus operation would see a number of changes, following developments within education systems.","In 1991, Navistar began its acquisition of AmTran (fully acquiring it in 1995), phasing out the Ward brand name after the 1992 model year. In 1992, Blue Bird would change hands for the first of several times. In 1998, Carpenter was acquired by Spartan Motors and Thomas Built Buses was sold to Freightliner; the latter was the final major school bus manufacturer operating under family control."]}
{"url":"https://www.schools.nyc.gov/school-life/transportation/transportation-overview","title":"Transportation Overview","description":"Curb-to-school or specialized bus service is provided to students whose medical, health, or disability-related conditions or special circumstances prevent them from being able to walk or utilize other forms of transportation to get to and from school. Student OMNY cards are provided at no cost to eligible students and can be used on MTA subways and buses (including Select Bus Service). Student OMNY cards are different from regular OMNY cards. A student OMNY card will have 4 trips and 4 transfers, and work at any time of the day, 7 days of the week.","extra_snippets":["Tab will move on to the next part of the site rather than go through menu items. ... Due to a shortage of available busing capacity, some yellow bus routes are still unassigned to vendors. We understand that this can be a challenge for families. To help ensure your child has transportation to school, NYCPS is offering free rideshare transportation through our vendor, LimoSys, as an alternative to yellow bus service.","Curb-to-school or specialized bus service is provided to students whose medical, health, or disability-related conditions or special circumstances prevent them from being able to walk or utilize other forms of transportation to get to and from school. Student OMNY cards are provided at no cost to eligible students and can be used on MTA subways and buses (including Select Bus Service). Student OMNY cards are different from regular OMNY cards. A student OMNY card will have 4 trips and 4 transfers, and work at any time of the day, 7 days of the week.","In partnership with the Metropolitan Transit Authority (MTA) and our school age and preschool bus companies, our mission is to ensure safe and reliable transportation to and from school. See Transportation Rights for what you and your child can expect from NYCPS transportation services.","NYCPS employees: visit Student Profile for student school and bus information or see Transportation Resources for Schools on the InfoHub for additional information. Sometimes buses are delayed because of traffic congestion or mechanical failure. Check our bus delays page to see if your child's route has been affected. Transportation services that are available to families vary by school and by each child’s eligibility for those services."]}
